  "generated_text": "To solve for \\( AC \\) in triangle \\( ABC \\) where \\( \\angle ABC = 90^\\circ \\), \\( AB = 12 \\), and \\( BD = 4 \\), we will use the Angle Bisector Theorem and the Pythagorean Theorem.\n\nFirst, let's denote the lengths of the sides of the triangle:\n- \\( AB = 12 \\)\n- \\( BD = 4 \\)\n- \\( DC = BC - BD = BC - 4 \\)\n\nSince \\( AD \\) is the angle bisector of \\( \\angle BAC \\), by the Angle Bisector Theorem, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{AB}{AC} = \\frac{BD}{DC}\n\\]\nLet \\( AC = x \\) and \\( BC = a \\). Then \\( DC = a - 4 \\). Substituting these into the Angle Bisector Theorem equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{12}{x} = \\frac{4}{a - 4}\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\( a \\) in terms of \\( x \\):\n\\[\n12(a - 4) = 4x\n\\]\n\\[\n12a - 48 = 4x\n\\]\n\\[\n3a - 12 = x\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 3a - 12\n\\]\n\nNow, we use the Pythagorean Theorem in triangle \\( ABC \\):\n\\[\nAB^2 + BC^2 = AC^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[\n12^2 + a^2 = x^2\n\\]\n\\[\n144 + a^2 = (3a - 12)^2\n\\]\nExpanding the right-hand side:\n\\[\n144 + a^2 = 9a^2 - 72a + 144\n\\]\nSubtract 144 from both sides:\n\\[\na^2 = 9a^2 - 72a\n\\]\nRearrange the equation:\n\\[\n0 = 8a^2 - 72a\n\\]\nFactor out \\( 8a \\):\n\\[\n0 = 8a(a - 9)\n\\]\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\na = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad a = 9\n\\]\nSince \\( a = 0 \\) is not possible, we have:\n\\[\na = 9\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( a = 9 \\) back into the equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[\nx = 3a - 12\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 3(9) - 12\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 27 - 12\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 15\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of \\( AC \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]",
